An API call is an HTTPS POST with JSON. For 3 real test messages:

```json
{
 "model": "<model-name>",
 "max_tokens": 200,
 "temperature": 0,
 "messages": [
  {
   "role": "system",
   "content": "You label SMS messages as spam or ham. Reply with JSON: {\"label\": \"spam\"|\"ham\", \"reason\": \"<10 words\"}."
  },
  {
   "role": "user",
   "content": "Messages:\n1. Very strange.  and  are watching the 2nd one now but i'm in bed. Sweet dreams, miss u \n2. Probably, want to pick up more?\n3. Have you started in skye"
  }
 ]
}
```

The API key goes in a header from an environment variable, never in code.

```python
app = FastAPI()
class Msg(BaseModel):
    text: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=1000)

@app.post('/predict')
def predict(m: Msg):
    p = model.predict_proba([m.text])[0, 1]
    return {'label': 'spam' if p > 0.5 else 'ham', 'p_spam': round(p, 3)}
```

The model (TF-IDF + logistic regression trained on real SMS) is loaded once at start-up, not per request.

```python
def tip_pct(bill, tip, nd=1):
    """Tip as a percentage of the bill."""
    return round(100 * tip / bill, nd)
```

`bill` and `tip` are parameters; `nd=1` has a default value.

```python
class Bill:
    def __init__(self, total, tip, day, size):
        self.total, self.tip, self.day, self.size = total, tip, day, size
    def tip_pct(self):
        return 100 * self.tip / self.total
    def per_person(self):
        return self.total / self.size
```
